<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Where a photo on its way in is allowed to be written.

    Two directories, both inside the app's own cache: one for a capture, one for
    the cropped copy of it. Neither activity can be handed a `file://` path — that
    has thrown FileUriExposedException since Android 7 — so each gets a
    `content://` URI from the provider that names this file, granted for one write
    and nothing else.

    Deliberately not the photo directory: what a camera produces and what the crop
    screen writes are full-resolution intermediates, and what the wardrobe keeps is
    a scaled copy. Each is read once, stored properly, and left for the system to
    reclaim.
-->
<paths>
    <cache-path name="camera" path="camera/" />
    <cache-path name="crop" path="crop/" />
    <!--
        And a downloaded build, on its way to Android's installer. In the cache for
        the same reason the others are: it is worth nothing once it is installed,
        and the system may reclaim it whenever it likes.
    -->
    <cache-path name="updates" path="updates/" />
</paths>
